<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sava River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sava is a river of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 950 kilometres long, which rises in the Julian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and flows east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and along the Bosnian border to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Its valley was a major route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, guarded in Roman times by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Siscia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>. For centuries it marked frontiers between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Balkan states and later between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and Ottoman empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sava.jpg" alt="Sava" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sava_River_and_Gazela_Bridge_(Belgrade).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sava is the longest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the largest by volume of water. Its headwater, the Sava Dolinka, rises in the Zelenci Pools in the Upper Carniola region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, at 833 metres above sea level, and meets the Sava Bohinjka near Radovljica. The river then crosses the Ljubljana Basin, flows into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>. Below the confluence of the Una it forms the border between Croatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, then turns north-east after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> joins and reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><p>It discharges into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at the Great War Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Its basin covers 97,713 square kilometres and drains much of the Dinaric Alps through tributaries such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a>, Bosna, Kupa, Una and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a>. The river's course is sometimes used to describe the northern boundary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the southern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. Before the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1991 it was the longest river lying completely within that country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name is believed to derive from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning to press or take liquid, so that it means that which waters the ground. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called the river Saos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> wrote of the Saüs, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used the name Savus. Worship of river gods in the area dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, when the first settlements were founded along the river. The Taurisci associated their river goddess Adsullata with the Savus, and altars or inscriptions to the river god Savus have been found at several places, among them the Zelenci Pools, Emona, Andautonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Siscia</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built settlements and forts along the Via Pannonia, the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Upstream of the Kupa confluence these included Emona, Andautonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Siscia</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, Velika Gorica and Sisak. Downstream stood Marsonia, built over a prehistoric settlement, then Cibalae, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> and Singidunum, on the sites of Slavonski Brod, Vinkovci, Sremska Mitrovica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 8,176,000 people live in the Sava basin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> stand on the river, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> in the basin. The river is navigable to larger vessels for 593.8 kilometres between Belgrade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a>, and its valley carries road and rail routes, among them Pan-European Corridor X. In Zagreb in 1964 the most destructive flood of the river put one third of the city under water and killed 17 people, after which a flood-relief canal to the Odra plain was built. The basin holds large alluvial wetlands, and six areas of it are protected under the Ramsar Convention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…6 March 1882 until his abdication on 6 March 1889, after the lost war and the total electoral victory of the People's Radical Party, which under <b>Sava</b> Grujić and Nikola Pašić had come to power in 1888 with a new constitution based on the liberal Constitution of Belgium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its 201 articles rested on Roman-Byzantine law and on St. <b>Sava</b>'s Nomocanon of 1219; articles 172 and 174, on judicial independence, were taken from the Byzantine Basilika.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cyrillic manuscripts, of the 11th century, include <b>Sava</b>’s book and the Codex Suprasliensis of 284 folios.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>A combined Christian army from the Habsburg lands, mainly the Kingdom of Croatia and Inner Austria, defeated Ottoman Bosnian forces at Sisak, where the <b>Sava</b> and Kupa rivers meet, on the borderland between Christian Europe and the Ottoman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1254 Béla IV of Hungary created a banate in the Mačva region south of the <b>Sava</b> and gave it to his son-in-law, the Rus prince Rostislav Mikhailovich, who also held Bosnian lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-macso">Banate of Macsó</a></li><li><blockquote>With war looming, the regent Prince Paul sought agreement with the Croatian Peasant Party, and the Cvetković-Maček Agreement of 26 August 1939 merged the <b>Sava</b> and Littoral banovinas with parts of Bosnia, Herzegovina, Srem and the Dubrovnik area into one Banovina of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/banovina-of-croatia">Banovina of Croatia</a></li></ul></section>
